<!--
  aihu-node-graph-background — presentational styled recipe (performativeUI
  port, Slice 10 — docs/plans/2026-08-01-performative-ui-port.md §Tier-C
  batch 2).

  Design and behavior transcribed in spirit from performativeUI (MIT); no
  source text or `pui-` class names vendored — see NOTICES.md. The upstream
  demo file was NOT read for this port: the effect below is written from the
  behavioral description in the slice brief plus the ordinary, widely-known
  "particle network" pattern.

  A decorative animated network/graph background — N small nodes drift across
  a managed canvas and any two nodes closer than `connection-distance` are
  joined by a line whose opacity fades to zero as that distance is
  approached (closer = more opaque).

  ── Architectural note: why this does NOT use `useParticleField` ──────────

  The obvious composition — "let `useParticleField` (Track B Slice 8) own the
  N drifting points and just add a line pass on top" — is not available, and
  the reason is in that composable's source, not a matter of taste:

    1. `useParticleField` has NO custom-draw hook. `UseParticleFieldOptions`
       exposes simulation knobs only (count, colors, radii, speed, gravity,
       pointerAttraction, twinkle, …). There is no `onFrame`/`draw` option
       and no flag to suppress its own painting: its private `draw()` is
       hard-wired into the single `onFrame` it hands `useCanvasSurface`, and
       that `draw()` opens with an unconditional
       `ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height)`. Anything this component painted
       onto that same surface would be erased at the top of the next frame.
    2. `particles()` is documented as the live backing array "exposed for
       tests and for effects that want to seed or perturb the field directly"
       — deliberately NOT reactive, because (its words) "the canvas IS the
       render". It is a state handle, not a render seam.
    3. So the only compositional route left is a SECOND `useCanvasSurface`
       for the line layer, reading `particles()` for positions. That buys a
       second `<canvas>`, a second rAF loop, and a second
       ResizeObserver/IntersectionObserver pair on one host — and, worse, two
       independently-scheduled paints: the line layer would render whatever
       positions the dot layer last integrated, so on any frame the two loops
       interleave, line endpoints visibly detach from the nodes they connect.
       Correctness here requires lines and nodes to be painted from ONE
       snapshot, in ONE frame, in ONE draw call sequence.

  Verdict: option (b) — drop to `useCanvasSurface` directly and hand-roll the
  minimal node drift. `useCanvasSurface` is explicitly "the substrate every
  canvas-based visual effect sits on … future canvas effects are expected to
  [sit on it] as well", so this is the sanctioned level to enter at, not a
  bypass. The duplicated logic is ~25 lines of Euler integration and
  edge-wrapping; every hard part — canvas ownership, HiDPI backing store,
  the resize dance that would otherwise feed a ResizeObserver loop, the
  off-screen pause, the rAF driver, the reduced-motion contract — is still
  inherited, not re-derived. Nothing is reimplemented that Slice 8 owns.

  ── Props ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  count:                how many nodes, string-encoded number. Default "40".
                        The pair pass is O(n²) per frame, which is the reason
                        this default is a few dozen and not a few hundred.
  color:                the single stroke/fill color for BOTH nodes and their
                        connecting lines. Default "#ffffff". This is a
                        LITERAL CSS color string, not a `--color-*` token
                        reference — identical constraint to
                        `floating-sparkles` (Slice 9): a canvas 2D context's
                        `fillStyle`/`strokeStyle` is not part of the CSS
                        cascade and cannot resolve a custom property, so it
                        needs an already-resolved value.
  connection-distance:  px threshold under which two nodes are joined,
                        string-encoded number. Default "120". (The prop is
                        declared `connectionDistance`; the runtime derives
                        the observed attribute as kebab-case, so consumers
                        write `connection-distance`.)

  ── Layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A standalone, full-bleed, absolutely-positioned decorative layer
  (`position: absolute; inset: 0`), no slot — the same "decorative background
  layer" contract `floating-sparkles` and `aurora` carry, for consistency
  across the Tier-C batch. The consumer places it as the first child of a
  `position: relative` ancestor and stacks real content above it with
  `z-index` (see `useCanvasSurface`'s header: "the host must be positioned"
  for its owned canvas to land at the host's top-left, which
  `position: absolute` already satisfies).

  ── Reduced motion / off-screen ──────────────────────────────────────────

  Neither is reimplemented here; both are `useCanvasSurface`'s job. Under
  `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` that composable's "paint once, never loop"
  contract calls `onFrame` exactly once with `reducedMotion: true` and never
  arms the rAF loop. `onFrame` below therefore skips `step()` and runs the
  identical `draw()` — so the frozen frame is a fully-populated graph at
  rest, nodes AND connections, never a blank box and never in motion.
  `isVisible`-gated pausing while scrolled off-screen is inherited for free.

  `shadow: 'light'` — see the parent execution plan's theming decision.
-->

@state {
  import { cn } from '@aihu/css-engine/runtime/cn'

  shadow: 'light'

  let count = prop({ default: '40' })
  let color = prop({ default: '#ffffff' })
  let connectionDistance = prop({ default: '120' })

  let hostEl = state(null)

  // Read every prop into a differently-named local before use — an
  // options-object key textually identical to a prop name trips the
  // compiler's prop-auto-unwrap rewriter (see token-stream.aihu's header for
  // the exact failure mode). Snapshotting here also matches the composable
  // family's convention: attributes are read once at setup, not live.
  const nodeCount = Math.max(0, Math.floor(Number(count) || 0))
  const inkColor = color
  const linkDistance = Math.max(0, Number(connectionDistance) || 0)

  const TAU = Math.PI * 2
  /** Peak drift speed, px/sec, seeded per node in [-SPEED, SPEED] per axis. */
  const SPEED = 14
  const NODE_RADIUS = 2
  const NODE_ALPHA = 0.85
  /** Alpha of a connection at zero distance; it fades linearly to 0 at
   * `linkDistance`. */
  const LINE_ALPHA = 0.45

  const nodes = []
  // Mutable scalars live on a const holder rather than as top-level `let`s:
  // `@state`'s reactive declarations are explicit (`state(...)`), and these
  // are deliberately plain frame-local bookkeeping that nothing renders from.
  const seeded = { width: 0, height: 0 }

  const seed = (width, height) => {
    nodes.length = 0
    for (let i = 0; i < nodeCount; i++) {
      nodes.push({
        x: Math.random() * width,
        y: Math.random() * height,
        vx: (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * SPEED,
        vy: (Math.random() * 2 - 1) * SPEED,
      })
    }
    seeded.width = width
    seeded.height = height
  }

  /** Integrate one step. `dt` is SECONDS, clamped for the same reason
   * `useParticleField` clamps: a backgrounded tab resumes with a multi-second
   * delta and integrating that unclamped teleports the whole graph away in a
   * single frame. Nodes WRAP rather than bounce — a bouncing graph reads as
   * trapped, a wrapping one reads as ambient drift. */
  const step = (dt, width, height) => {
    const clamped = dt > 0.05 ? 0.05 : dt
    for (const n of nodes) {
      n.x += n.vx * clamped
      n.y += n.vy * clamped
      if (n.x < -NODE_RADIUS) n.x += width + NODE_RADIUS * 2
      else if (n.x > width + NODE_RADIUS) n.x -= width + NODE_RADIUS * 2
      if (n.y < -NODE_RADIUS) n.y += height + NODE_RADIUS * 2
      else if (n.y > height + NODE_RADIUS) n.y -= height + NODE_RADIUS * 2
    }
  }

  /** Connections first, then nodes, so the dots sit ON TOP of the web rather
   * than being bisected by every line that terminates on them. */
  const draw = (ctx, width, height) => {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, width, height)

    if (linkDistance > 0) {
      const maxSq = linkDistance * linkDistance
      ctx.strokeStyle = inkColor
      ctx.lineWidth = 1
      for (let i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) {
        const a = nodes[i]
        for (let j = i + 1; j < nodes.length; j++) {
          const b = nodes[j]
          const dx = a.x - b.x
          const dy = a.y - b.y
          const distSq = dx * dx + dy * dy
          // Compare SQUARED distances to reject the vast majority of pairs
          // without a sqrt; only survivors pay for the real distance, which
          // the opacity ramp needs.
          if (distSq >= maxSq) continue
          ctx.globalAlpha = LINE_ALPHA * (1 - Math.sqrt(distSq) / linkDistance)
          ctx.beginPath()
          ctx.moveTo(a.x, a.y)
          ctx.lineTo(b.x, b.y)
          ctx.stroke()
        }
      }
    }

    ctx.globalAlpha = NODE_ALPHA
    ctx.fillStyle = inkColor
    for (const n of nodes) {
      ctx.beginPath()
      ctx.arc(n.x, n.y, NODE_RADIUS, 0, TAU)
      ctx.fill()
    }
    // Leave the context as we found it — a later `redraw()` must not inherit
    // the last node's alpha.
    ctx.globalAlpha = 1
  }

  /** Re-seed only on the FIRST real size; re-randomizing on every resize tick
   * would make a window drag look like static. Later sizes rescale
   * proportionally so no node is stranded outside the new box. */
  const handleResize = (width, height) => {
    if (nodes.length === 0) {
      seed(width, height)
      return
    }
    const sx = seeded.width > 0 ? width / seeded.width : 1
    const sy = seeded.height > 0 ? height / seeded.height : 1
    for (const n of nodes) {
      n.x *= sx
      n.y *= sy
    }
    seeded.width = width
    seeded.height = height
  }

  const handleFrame = (frame) => {
    if (nodes.length === 0) seed(frame.width, frame.height)
    // The reduced-motion contract: paint the graph, never integrate it.
    if (!frame.reducedMotion) step(frame.delta / 1000, frame.width, frame.height)
    draw(frame.ctx, frame.width, frame.height)
  }

  // Both callbacks are hoisted to named consts rather than written inline as
  // `onResize: (w, h) => { … }`. An options-object entry whose VALUE is a
  // multi-line arrow function is mis-parsed by the compiler's @state
  // macro/prop rewriter — it reads the `key:` as a state-macro declaration
  // and emits `let onResize: (width, height) => {`, which is not valid TS
  // (`Expected "}" but found "onResize"`). Same family of rewriter hazard as
  // the prop-name/option-key collision documented in token-stream.aihu; the
  // fix is the same shape — keep the object literal's values to bare
  // identifiers whose names differ from their keys.
  useCanvasSurface(() => hostEl, {
    onResize: handleResize,
    onFrame: handleFrame,
  })
}

@template {
  <div
    ref={hostEl}
    data-slot="node-graph-background"
    aria-hidden="true"
    class={cn('aihu-node-graph-background')}></div>
}

@style {
  .aihu-node-graph-background {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
}
